      "nets": ["GND"],
      "observed": "In the netlist, U10.4 (GND1) and U10.5 (GND2) are both on net GND; same for U11.4 and U11.5.",
      "expected": "GND1 (logic side) and GND2 (isolated side) should be separate nets to maintain galvanic isolation.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "GND [High Power] : ... U10.4(GND1,power_in) U10.5(GND2,power_in) ... U11.4(GND1,power_in) U11.5(GND2,power_in) ..."
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "two isolated CAN buses (CAN0, CAN1), each driven by its own MCP2515 SPI CAN controller (U8, U9) through an isolated CAN transceiver (U11/companion)"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "Loss of galvanic isolation, defeating the purpose of isolated CAN transceivers, potential ground loops and damage.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["R34", "R35", "SW6"],
      "pins": ["R34.1", "R34.2", "R35.1", "R35.2", "SW6.1", "SW6.2", "SW6.3", "SW6.4"],
      "nets": ["/CAN0_N", "/CAN0_P", "/CAN1_N", "/CAN1_P", "Net-(R34-Pad2)", "Net-(R35-Pad2)"],
      "observed": "R34 is between /CAN0_N and Net-(R34-Pad2) which goes to SW6.1; R35 is between /CAN1_N and Net-(R35-Pad2) which goes to SW6.2. SW6.1-2 can connect the two resistor ends together. SW6.3 is on /CAN1_P and SW6.4 is on /CAN0_P, so closing SW6.3-4 shorts CAN0_P to CAN1_P.",
      "expected": "Termination resistors should be connected between CANH and CANL of each bus (e.g., R34 between /CAN0_P and /CAN0_N, R35 between /CAN1_P and /CAN1_N), with switches in series to enable termination. The two buses should remain isolated.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "/CAN0_N : ... R34.1 ... /CAN0_P : ... SW6.4 ... /CAN1_N : ... R35.1 ... /CAN1_P : ... SW6.3 ... Net-(R34-Pad2) : R34.2 SW6.1 Net-(R35-Pad2) : R35.2 SW6.2"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "No proper CAN termination; closing SW6.3-4 shorts the two CAN buses' positive lines, defeating isolation and causing bus contention.",
      "confidence": 0.9
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["U15", "U22", "U35", "U36"],
      "pins": ["U22.2", "U22.4", "U15.2", "U15.4", "U35.2", "U35.4", "U36.1", "U36.2", "U36.4"],
      "nets": ["ESTOP_SINK1", "ESTOP_SINK2", "ESTOP_SINK_LOCAL", "/ESTOP_SINK_ALL", "Net-(U15-A)", "Net-(U15-Y)", "ESTOP", "ESTOP_OUT"],
      "observed": "U22 input A is tied to GND; its output Y drives U15 input A. U15 output Y drives U35 input A. U35 output Y is /ESTOP_SINK_ALL. This creates a series chain where /ESTOP_SINK_ALL is pulled low only if all three /OE inputs (ESTOP_SINK1, ESTOP_SINK2, ESTOP_SINK_LOCAL) are low.",
      "expected": "The e-stop sinks should be wired as a wired-OR (all outputs tied together to /ESTOP_SINK_ALL, all inputs tied to GND) so that any single sink going low activates the e-stop.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "GND : ... U22.2(A,input) ... Net-(U15-A) : ... U15.2(A,input) U22.4(Y,output) ... Net-(U15-Y) : ... U15.4(Y,output) U35.2(A,input) ... /ESTOP_SINK_ALL : ... U35.4(Y,output) U36.1(/OE,input)"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "e-stop should activate if a cable is disconnected. - nevermind, it does this"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "E-stop only activates if all three sink points are low simultaneously; a single cable disconnect will not trigger the e-stop, compromising safety.",
      "confidence": 0.85
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design files contain multiple anomalies. The most critical is that the isolated CAN transceivers have their logic and isolated grounds tied together, defeating galvanic isolation. Additionally, the CAN termination network is miswired, shorting the two CAN buses' positive lines, and the e-stop chain is configured as an AND instead of an OR, so a single cable disconnect will not trigger the e-stop."
}

